---
import Button from '../../components/Button.astro';
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../../lib/siteUrl';
import StatusPill from '../../components/StatusPill.astro';
---

<Layout title="Stroke System" description="Varve combines editable vector contours with raster sketching, inking, clipped shading, and transparent export in the existing Design workspace.">
  <div class="container-custom">
    <div class="feature-page">
      <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href={sitePath('/features')}>Features</a> / Stroke System
      </nav>

      <div class="feature-heading">
        <StatusPill status="available" />
        <h1>Stroke System</h1>
        <p class="feature-intro">
          Keep vector outlines editable and add raster brush marks on the same canvas. Strokes remain ordered document data instead of flattening the artwork.
        </p>
      </div>

      <section class="feature-section">
        <h2>What you can edit</h2>
        <div class="capability-grid">
          <article>
            <h3>Geometry-aware outlines</h3>
            <p>Center, inside, and outside alignment for enclosed shapes; open paths keep a truthful centered stroke. Curved closing segments and compound contours stay in the replay path.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <h3>Paint and stack</h3>
            <p>Use solid or spatial gradient paint, multiple ordered stroke entries, width, visibility, caps, joins, miter limits, dash phase, and dash presets with exact custom patterns. The collapsed Advanced row names every non-default setting, new strokes copy the layer's previous style, and a zero-width stroke is flagged as invisible.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <h3>Paths and markers</h3>
            <p>Open paths can carry independent start and end markers. Curved endpoints use their local tangent, so markers follow the path rather than its overall chord.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <h3>Text and image borders</h3>
            <p>Editable text uses glyph strokes in the live Canvas2D route. Image strokes remain frame borders; transparent-subject silhouettes are a separate explicit operation.</p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="feature-section">
        <h2>Variable width without segment darkening</h2>
        <p>
          Pen and pencil paths can retain per-point pressure. The renderer evaluates that profile as one filled outline so translucent widths do not darken where tessellated segments overlap. Pressure 0.5 is neutral, 0 tapers to zero, and missing samples use the neutral profile.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="feature-section">
        <h2>Raster marks on the same canvas</h2>
        <p>
          Vector strokes and raster marks share the existing editor and document. Switch to Paint, Eraser, or Smudge when the artwork needs pixel-level texture, line cleanup, or a soft paintover; no separate illustration document is required.
        </p>
        <div class="capability-grid">
          <article>
            <h3>Predictable smudge modes</h3>
            <p>Choose pure smudge, loaded-paint, or fingerpaint behavior in Tool options. The reservoir is frozen per stroke so a setting change cannot rewrite an in-progress mark.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <h3>Starting brushes for line and tone</h3>
            <p>Choose Sketch Pencil, Inking Nib, Opaque Paint, or Soft Shade, then refine pressure, size, flow, and accumulation in the existing brush controls. Soft Shade limits one gesture to its opacity while flow controls buildup; separate strokes can deepen it. Browser previews use the production stroke generator and tile compositor.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <h3>Explicit stack sampling</h3>
            <p>Opt into merged raster-layer sampling when texturing a new paint layer. Deposits still go to the active target, keeping the source layers intact.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <h3>Selection-to-flats</h3>
            <p>Sample one raster or the visible artwork with Magic Wand, create a separate Flats layer, and fill color beneath your linework. The outline stays editable; undo, save, reopen, and grouped PNG export preserve the combined result.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <h3>Clipped shading</h3>
            <p>In Design, select a visible raster layer and create a separate Shading layer clipped to its alpha. The live matte keeps paint inside the source silhouette, while edits remain undoable and survive save/reopen and transparent PNG export.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <h3>Paint stays on its target</h3>
            <p>With no layer selected, Paint uses an eligible pixel layer on the active page or creates a named Brush Layer on a blank canvas. An explicit vector or object selection never redirects a stroke to another layer; Varve refuses it and announces how to add a paint layer.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <h3>Honest boundaries</h3>
            <p>Current layer sampling stays available when the selection should come from one raster. Visible artwork uses the rendered active surface as its source, while fills still write to one selected pixel layer. Edge expansion grows coverage by up to 8 source pixels under antialiased linework. Contiguous visible-artwork sampling can also close short contour gaps with a bounded 0–8 px radius; larger openings may still reach the exterior, so inspect the selection before filling.</p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </section>

      <figure class="feature-proof">
        <img
          src={sitePath('/screenshots/illustration-linework-flats.png')}
          alt="Varve showing a red apple flat beneath editable black linework on a separate Flats layer"
          width="1280"
          height="800"
        />
        <figcaption>
          Visible-artwork Magic Wand selects inside the ink while a separate Flats layer carries the
          color. Undo, save and reopen, and grouped PNG export were verified in the editor.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <figure class="feature-proof">
        <img
          src={sitePath('/screenshots/illustration-clipped-shading.png')}
          alt="Varve Design workspace with red shading clipped inside a blue painted shape on a separate Shading layer"
          width="1440"
          height="900"
        />
        <figcaption>
          The separate Shading layer stays within the source raster alpha. The real editor
          workflow was checked for boundary pixels, undo/redo, save/reopen, and transparent PNG.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <figure class="feature-proof">
        <img
          src={sitePath('/screenshots/illustration-vector-clipped-texture.png')}
          alt="Varve Design workspace showing a red raster shading stroke clipped inside a teal vector contour"
          width="1440"
          height="900"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>
          A vector contour and raster shading share one editable document. The verified SVG keeps
          supported contour geometry native and embeds the clipped texture as a bounded raster
          fallback; browser PDF preserves the appearance through raster export.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <section class="feature-section limitations">
        <h2>Export boundaries</h2>
        <p>
          PNG/raster export uses the full Canvas2D replay. SVG keeps supported center strokes, gradients, dashes, miter limits, markers, and editable text strokes native; alignment, asymmetric borders, pressure-variable paths, image borders, frames, and stacked stroke cases use a bounded raster fallback when required for fidelity. PDF applies the same honest fallback for features its native path writer cannot represent.
        </p>
        <p>
          Read the <a href={sitePath('/docs/tools/strokes')}>Stroke guide</a> for the target matrix and the <a href={sitePath('/features/export')}>Export &amp; Code Generation</a> guide for broader format behavior.
        </p>
      </section>

      <div class="feature-cta">
        <Button href={sitePath('/docs/tools/strokes')}>Read the stroke guide</Button>
        <Button variant="secondary" href={sitePath('/docs/tools/object-selection')}>
          Read the Object Selection guide
        </Button>
        <Button variant="secondary" href={sitePath('/features/vector-tools')}>Explore vector tools</Button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .feature-page {
    max-width: 832px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 3rem 0;
  }

  .breadcrumb {
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }

  .breadcrumb a { color: var(--text-secondary); }

  .feature-heading { margin-bottom: 3rem; }

  .feature-heading h1 {
    margin: 0.75rem 0 1rem;
    font-family: var(--font-editorial);
    font-size: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4rem);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: -0.025em;
  }

  .feature-intro {
    max-width: 680px;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .feature-section { margin-bottom: 3rem; }

  .feature-section h2 {
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--border-accent);
    padding-bottom: 0.5rem;
    font-size: 1.6rem;
  }

  .feature-section p, .capability-grid p {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    line-height: 1.75;
  }

  .capability-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-5);
  }

  .capability-grid article {
    border: 1px solid var(--border-default);
    border-radius: var(--website-radius-card);
    padding: 1.25rem;
    background: var(--surface-card);
  }

  .capability-grid h3 { margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }

  .feature-proof {
    margin: 0 0 3rem;
  }

  .feature-proof img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--border-default);
    border-radius: var(--website-radius-card);
  }

  .feature-proof figcaption {
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .limitations {
    border-left: 3px solid var(--border-accent);
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
  }

  .feature-cta {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border-default);
    padding-top: 2rem;
  }
</style>
